    {
      "id": "9b167171-ebb1-4aac-9170-d28adcaed89b",
      "name": "Sticky Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        0,
        0
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 480,
        "height": 1040,
        "content": "## Attack Surface Change Monitor\n\n### How it works\n\nThis workflow periodically scans configured external discovery sources to monitor changes in an organization’s attack surface. It combines subdomain, exposed-service, and cloud-inventory results, normalizes and fingerprints them, then compares the current inventory against a stored Postgres baseline to find new, changed, and removed assets. Detected changes are risk-scored, enriched with an AI-generated triage narrative, checked for unauthorized cloud footprint, and routed to Slack or email based on severity while the current inventory is upserted as the latest baseline.\n\n### Setup steps\n\n- Configure the schedule trigger for the desired scan frequency.\n- Update the configuration node with root domains and the URLs for the subdomain, exposed-services, and cloud-inventory APIs.\n- Set up credentials and schema/table access for the Postgres baseline read and upsert queries.\n- Configure OpenAI credentials for the AI risk triage agent and model sub-node.\n- Configure SMTP/email credentials and recipients for compliance notifications and security digests.\n- Configure Slack credentials, target workspace, and alert channel for immediate high-priority notifications.\n- Review the code nodes for asset normalization, fingerprinting, deduplication, policy checks, and severity scoring to ensure they match the organization’s asset model.\n\n### Customization\n\nAdjust the discovery APIs, asset fingerprint fields, allowed cloud providers/regions, severity thresholds, and notification routing rules to match internal security policy and escalation processes."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
